SUMMARY:Iftikhar Dadi | Romanticism and Politics in Mid-Century Lahore Ci
 nema
LOCATION:Louis A. Simpson International Building\, A71
DESCRIPTION:Mid-century commercial cinema in South Asia has served as amo
 ng the most powerful vectors of social and aesthetic modernization. By a
 nchoring social change across deeper cultural imaginaries\, this cinema 
 has played an influential progressive role by constituting publics beyon
 d existing social divides\, forging a shared and expanded experience of 
 modernity that extends beyond regional\, ethnic\, and sectarian affiliat
 ions\, and affectively challenging the selective amnesia of nation-state
  ideologies. In this talk\, Iftikhar Dadi focuses on the contributions o
 f Khurshid Anwar (1912-84)\, who worked in Bombay cinema and later in La
 hore cinema. His music\, writing\, and direction in Lahore ostensibly ce
 nter on the conflict between the “East” and the “West.” However\
 , Anwar renders this tension distinctive through the role music plays in
  inviting healing from the unbearable implications of this divide\nAudie
 nce: Open to the Public\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/iftikhar-
 dadi-romanticism-and-politics-mid-century-lahore-cinema
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/iftikhar-dadi-romanticism-and-p
 olitics-mid-century-lahore-cinema
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:60bf00197d9dc1dff1371e0c96cd094d
DTSTAMP:20260920T025819Z
DTSTART:20260929T203000Z
DTEND:20260929T213000Z
SUMMARY:Jenny’s Worlds: Asian Lives and the Archives of American Empire
 \, Nadine Attewell
LOCATION:East Pyne Building\, 010
DESCRIPTION:In November 1969\, American serviceman Jerry Harlowe spent se
 ven days on rest and recreation leave in Hong Kong\, where he took numer
 ous photographs of a “Hong Kong prostitute” named “Jenny.” While
  Harlowe’s archive bears witness to the possibilities of US empire for
  masculine pleasure\, his photographs of Jenny show a self-assured young
  woman with style to burn. They also testify to the political arrangemen
 ts of dependency that bound American imperial infrastructures of social 
 reproduction to both Hong Kong and women like Jenny. In this talk\, I wi
 ll reflect on the affordances of American photographic archives like Har
 lowe’s as sites of encounter with the many Asian women whose reproduct
 ive labour\, including domestic\, clerical\, entertainment\, and sex wor
 k at once sustained and disrupted US dominance across east and southeast
  Asia during the early decades of the Cold War. Asian women’s parti\nA
 udience: Open to the Public\, Faculty & Academic Professionals\, Staff\,
  Students\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/jennys-worlds-asian-liv
 es-and-archives-american-empire-nadine-attewell
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/jennys-worlds-asian-lives-and-a
 rchives-american-empire-nadine-attewell
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
