SUMMARY:Performance and Contradiction: Acting Natural
LOCATION:Art Museum\, 134 Small Auditorium
DESCRIPTION:In the 1970s\, performance gained widespread purchase as a di
 screte artistic category and lens of scholarly analysis in the United St
 ates. At the same time\, its discourses were mired in irresolution. This
  talk argues that these twinned phenomena issue from performance’s for
 mal capacity to register contradictions at the level of political econom
 y. I take artists as among the first and best theorists of the sharp ris
 e in waged affective labor as it collided with long-standing social mand
 ates for authenticity—both gendered and racialized—to offer a new hi
 storical account of performance’s dogged resistance to straightforward
  definition. With particular attention to the roles into which Black fem
 ininity is routinely conscripted\, I interrogate the era’s shifting un
 derstandings of “neutral” (that is\, “expressionless”) performin
 g styles\, mobilizing archival research and reception history to conte\n
 Audience: Open to the Public\, Faculty & Academic Professionals\, Staff\
 , Students\, Alumni\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/performance-a
 nd-contradiction-acting-natural
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/performance-and-contradiction-a
 cting-natural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
