SUMMARY:HMEI Faculty Seminar: "How to Sink a Megacity: A Histoy of Water\
 , Land\, and Environmental Change in Mexico City" by Vera Candiani
LOCATION:Briger Hall\, C112 Lecture Hall
DESCRIPTION:Vera Candiani\, Associate Professor of History\, will present
  “How to Sink a Megacity: A History of Water\, Land\, and Environmenta
 l Change in Mexico City” Join us in the Briger Hall Auditorium\, 11 Iv
 y Lane\, or stream it live. Candiani is the second speaker in the Fall 2
 026 HMEI Faculty Seminar Series. Modern Mexico City’s chronic flooding
 \, aquifer depletion\, and land subsidence are the cumulative result of 
 five centuries during which land speculators\, financiers\, state engine
 ers\, and political authorities transformed a lake basin into a megacity
  whose very development generated the conditions for today’s water cri
 sis. This talk traces how\, from the seventeenth century onward\, succes
 sive projects to drain the lakes\, control water\, and expand the city r
 eshaped the basin’s hydrology while allocating environmental costs and
  benefits along class lines. By placing this process in historical persp
 \nAudience: Open to the Public\, Faculty & Academic Professionals\, Staf
 f\, Students\, Alumni\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/hmei-facult
 y-seminar-how-sink-megacity-histoy-water-land-and-environmental-change
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/hmei-faculty-seminar-how-sink-m
 egacity-histoy-water-land-and-environmental-change
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
